00:14:46,393 --> 00:14:47,974
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, the disgusting nature of the body.

183
00:14:48,714 --> 00:14:49,394
[SPEAKER_01]: On that level.

184
00:14:49,454 --> 00:14:49,794
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, yeah.

185
00:14:50,095 --> 00:14:55,177
[SPEAKER_02]: Only on the basis of thorough restraint and then discerning this body that endures while I'm restrained.

186
00:14:55,317 --> 00:14:55,997
[SPEAKER_02]: You have to see it.

187
00:14:56,077 --> 00:15:06,081
[SPEAKER_02]: It's not just... because if people otherwise just take up contemplation of the disgusting body while their mind is still on the level of sense objects, that's not the body they're contemplating.

188
00:15:06,101 --> 00:15:07,342
[SPEAKER_02]: They're just gonna, like,

189
00:15:08,262 --> 00:15:10,563
[SPEAKER_02]: They're contemplating it through perverted order, still.

190
00:15:10,943 --> 00:15:12,424
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it doesn't apply to what it should apply.

191
00:15:12,444 --> 00:15:26,490
[SPEAKER_03]: If you contemplate the body, the disgusting body, while not taking it up, having not taken it up, or... Having not taken it up to sense restraint and guarding of the sense doors, not for a night or two, but for much longer.

192
00:15:26,670 --> 00:15:27,611
[SPEAKER_02]: It takes time to dry up.

193
00:15:27,671 --> 00:15:34,254
[SPEAKER_02]: It takes time for that aggregator virtue to sort of gain weight, so to speak, and be palpable there.

194
00:15:35,354 --> 00:15:36,535
[SPEAKER_01]: But you would kind of recognize

195
00:15:37,767 --> 00:15:52,040
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, if you're honest, whether you're doing it right or not, whether the contemplation of the disgusting nature is kind of on the level of objects, and it's kind of like secondary, or whether it's actually undermining the possibility of...

196
00:15:52,100 --> 00:15:54,943
[SPEAKER_02]: But the thing is, even the honesty comes up with withdrawal.

197
00:15:55,243 --> 00:15:59,647
[SPEAKER_02]: So, if people are not withdrawn, they can think they're honest, but they might not necessarily be honest.

198
00:16:00,248 --> 00:16:01,549
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's why first things first.

199
00:16:02,870 --> 00:16:17,059
[SPEAKER_02]: Presence and guarding of the sense doors, not distracting yourself and overcoming concern towards the world, joys and concerns, not dealing with the world, not being engaged with it, not being actively emotionally tied to it.

200
00:16:17,179 --> 00:16:25,044
[SPEAKER_02]: The pleasure of withdrawal would be determined by what you will be drawn from, by the peril, by the weight, by the pain that you used to have on account of it.

201
00:16:25,884 --> 00:16:26,084
[SPEAKER_02]: Right?

202
00:16:26,104 --> 00:16:28,947
[SPEAKER_02]: Because, see, that's always, it's always at the back of your mind.

203
00:16:29,908 --> 00:16:42,062
[SPEAKER_02]: Every time you borrow, every time you take up that, to that extent, the uncertainty, you become liable to uncertainty, to loss.

204
00:16:43,737 --> 00:16:44,578
[SPEAKER_02]: To more pressure.

205
00:16:44,678 --> 00:16:45,858
[SPEAKER_02]: And you know that every time.

206
00:16:46,118 --> 00:16:46,679
[SPEAKER_02]: To sickness.

207
00:16:46,939 --> 00:16:47,919
[SPEAKER_02]: To sickness, every time.

208
00:16:47,959 --> 00:16:52,021
[SPEAKER_02]: So, to the extent that you're taking things up to... from... from... from here.

209
00:16:52,182 --> 00:17:01,827
[SPEAKER_02]: So, I'm taking up... Basically, I'm relying to sense pleasures to... to avoid the pressure of the body, the... the fatigue of the senses, the... the pain of... of sense restraint.

210
00:17:02,907 --> 00:17:11,854
[SPEAKER_02]: I know each time, to the extent I take, to that extent, I remain connected to uncertainty, to pain, to loss, to liability, to dukkha of any kind.

211
00:17:13,075 --> 00:17:18,540
[SPEAKER_02]: But if you don't take it up, at any, on any level, and relinquish that which pressures you, i.e.

212
00:17:18,560 --> 00:17:23,864
[SPEAKER_02]: the body, that natural fatigue we spoke about before, then you realize, oh, this is truly blameless.

213
00:17:24,705 --> 00:17:25,265
[SPEAKER_02]: I'm...

214
00:17:25,285 --> 00:17:28,948
[SPEAKER_02]: I've relinquished the pain, I've relinquished the debt, without incurring more debt.

215
00:17:30,225 --> 00:17:35,809
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's... And you would feel that at the back of your mind, that, that, well, that release.

216
00:17:37,250 --> 00:17:43,034
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's a release that doesn't depend on doing anything...

217
00:17:43,054 --> 00:17:43,414
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

218
00:17:43,434 --> 00:17:46,116
[SPEAKER_00]: ...or judging your circumstances in any level.

219
00:17:46,136 --> 00:17:49,819
[SPEAKER_02]: That's the way... And that's exactly why you did not incur more debt by doing it.

220
00:17:50,339 --> 00:17:51,580
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's what Buddha recognized.

221
00:17:51,600 --> 00:17:57,304
[SPEAKER_02]: Wait, why am I afraid of this pleasure that is blameless, that does not have more debt as its base?

222
00:17:59,031 --> 00:18:00,032
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, let me then pursue it.

223
00:18:00,072 --> 00:18:05,715
[SPEAKER_02]: So, he pursued the pleasure of vāsagā, as we spoke before, the pleasure of relinquishing.

224
00:18:06,596 --> 00:18:12,980
[SPEAKER_02]: But relinquishing needs to be done on the level of that which you're not relinquishing, which is your body.

225
00:18:13,360 --> 00:18:15,161
[SPEAKER_02]: You can't just go around... Well, you can.

226
00:18:15,641 --> 00:18:19,424
[SPEAKER_02]: I suppose it would be a useful practice in the beginning, but that's not what we're talking about.

227
00:18:19,804 --> 00:18:28,129
[SPEAKER_02]: We're talking about relinquishing that necessary basis for your state of debt, for your state of peril.

228
00:18:29,572 --> 00:18:32,774
[SPEAKER_02]: For the liability of dukkha, and that is your relationship with your own body.

229
00:18:32,814 --> 00:18:33,715
[SPEAKER_02]: That's what you're taking up.

230
00:18:34,455 --> 00:18:50,766
[SPEAKER_02]: That patiga, that discomfort of having this lump of flesh, this foam, you need to relinquish, having not being incurring, having not sort of constantly trying to cover it up by incurring more debt of sensuality and sensual pleasures.

231
00:18:51,827 --> 00:18:55,950
[SPEAKER_02]: But then, yes, relinquishing it would equal the release.

232
00:18:58,165 --> 00:18:59,005
[SPEAKER_02]: Because you're not doing anything.

233
00:18:59,025 --> 00:19:03,346
[SPEAKER_02]: You're just giving up that... you're just not taking what you used to take, what you're usually taking up.

234
00:19:04,766 --> 00:19:06,566
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it's not like you're replacing it with something else.

235
00:19:07,187 --> 00:19:07,847
[SPEAKER_02]: You're giving it up.

236
00:19:10,547 --> 00:19:15,748
[SPEAKER_02]: And that is the kind of, you could say, that's a prerequisite for jhāna, that vāsaka, that giving up, relinquishment.

237
00:19:16,548 --> 00:19:19,629
[SPEAKER_02]: Contemplating relinquishment, the mind would enter the first jhāna.

238
00:19:20,949 --> 00:19:23,669
[SPEAKER_02]: Obviously, relinquishment, as I said, of the right things on the right level.

239
00:19:25,490 --> 00:19:26,590
[SPEAKER_02]: So, what is that body then?

240
00:19:27,585 --> 00:19:35,072
[SPEAKER_02]: Say somebody is properly restrained, withdrawn, overcoming concern, joys towards the world, not distracting themselves.

241
00:19:36,473 --> 00:19:42,338
[SPEAKER_02]: How would you describe, from the internal point of view, you and the relationship with this body?

242
00:19:42,838 --> 00:19:43,219
[SPEAKER_02]: What is it?

243
00:19:43,539 --> 00:19:44,039
[SPEAKER_02]: How is it?

244
00:19:44,380 --> 00:19:45,140
[SPEAKER_02]: How is it found?

245
00:19:47,462 --> 00:19:48,423
[SPEAKER_02]: How do you not take it up?

246
00:19:48,483 --> 00:19:49,324
[SPEAKER_02]: How do you relinquish it?

247
00:19:51,287 --> 00:19:58,471
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, what I'm saying is, sensory strength, withdrawal, will kind of put you in a position to discern this body, but you still have to discern it.

248
00:20:01,954 --> 00:20:08,838
[SPEAKER_02]: For as long as a monk is not developed in regard to his own body, he's not free from sensuality.

249
00:20:09,038 --> 00:20:13,080
[SPEAKER_02]: So, how is one developed in regard to his own body?

250
00:20:13,100 --> 00:20:14,061
[SPEAKER_02]: How do you develop that?

251
00:20:25,540 --> 00:20:29,244
[SPEAKER_04]: Like Alex was saying, there's a lot of disgust in regards to that.

252
00:20:29,444 --> 00:20:30,605
[SPEAKER_04]: We're seeing it on the right level.

253
00:20:30,925 --> 00:20:34,128
[SPEAKER_02]: Seeing the disgust, practicing the subha on the right level, yeah.

254
00:20:34,809 --> 00:20:36,811
[SPEAKER_02]: So...

255
00:20:36,851 --> 00:20:37,732
[SPEAKER_00]: Thinking about wash.

256
00:20:38,152 --> 00:20:40,334
[SPEAKER_00]: It is wash. Yeah, yeah.

257
00:20:40,554 --> 00:20:41,635
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, it's very obvious.

258
00:20:41,675 --> 00:20:42,356
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, that's the thing.

259
00:20:42,396 --> 00:20:42,717
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

260
00:20:42,737 --> 00:20:56,713
[SPEAKER_02]: So, when you are withdrawn from sensuality, when you are not approaching your own body through the perverted order of your own senses first, of your sense objects first, you would, even here, just contemplate in skin.

261
00:20:57,814 --> 00:21:11,949
[SPEAKER_02]: Head, ears, teeth, literally, they're just things like these stones and soil and wood and grass, just in a particular pile, but same, you know, calcium and tissue and water and elements, that's it.

262
00:21:12,770 --> 00:21:13,130
[SPEAKER_02]: That's it.

263
00:21:13,290 --> 00:21:15,813
[SPEAKER_02]: Pile of stone, pile of wood, pile of flesh.

264
00:21:17,515 --> 00:21:19,157
[SPEAKER_02]: So, from that

265
00:21:20,732 --> 00:21:33,903
[SPEAKER_02]: Peripheral point of view, as I said, the one that you have been withdrawn from, and you see your body there now as one of these things, that's like, yeah, that's... you don't need to do more to then discern the unknown ability of it, and so on.

266
00:21:33,943 --> 00:21:35,484
[SPEAKER_02]: You just need to protect it, maintain it.

267
00:21:36,665 --> 00:21:45,473
[SPEAKER_02]: And then also, as I said before, contemplate the danger of losing the sight of the body on that level, which basically equals losing mindfulness.

268
00:21:46,848 --> 00:21:55,650
[SPEAKER_02]: Because most people practice mindfulness through the perverted order of sense objects first, and then I feel my sensations and watch this as objects of my experience.

269
00:21:55,670 --> 00:22:10,613
[SPEAKER_02]: Now, you've got to withdraw yourself from all of that, stop engaging with it, to discern this body that's already here, necessary basis for any direction that I'm activating myself and doing it, and then start seeing that body that endures there, whether I think about it or not.

270
00:22:13,461 --> 00:22:21,506
[SPEAKER_02]: That's completely unownable, given, outside of, like, I did not create it, shape it, form it, and I can certainly not step outside of it.

271
00:22:22,227 --> 00:22:27,831
[SPEAKER_02]: Yet, my whole perception, feeling, my whole intent, my whole experience, my whole being, depends on the existence of it.

272
00:22:27,971 --> 00:22:33,875
[SPEAKER_02]: Because if these elements that compose this body would just disintegrate, that's it.

273
00:22:33,915 --> 00:22:37,177
[SPEAKER_02]: The aggregates could not remain, the rest of the aggregates could not remain standing.

274
00:22:39,035 --> 00:22:58,888
[SPEAKER_02]: So, if those contemplations... See, anybody can think these things, but if they're not resulting in release, in kind of furthering of the release, in furthering of kind of losing the desire towards sense objects, then you're not contemplating on the right level.

275
00:22:59,966 --> 00:23:03,609
[SPEAKER_02]: That means you're not... you still haven't taken your sticks even out of the water.

276
00:23:04,210 --> 00:23:04,890
[SPEAKER_02]: You're not restrained.

277
00:23:04,930 --> 00:23:05,811
[SPEAKER_02]: You're not restrained.

278
00:23:05,951 --> 00:23:10,615
[SPEAKER_02]: You might be somewhat restrained, or you might be more restrained than you used to be, but that's still not sufficient.

279
00:23:11,857 --> 00:23:17,021
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, because if you are restrained, then it would actually kind of be hard not to do it on the right level.

280
00:23:17,041 --> 00:23:17,542
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what I mean.

281
00:23:17,562 --> 00:23:18,202
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, that's what I mean.

282
00:23:18,222 --> 00:23:22,206
[SPEAKER_02]: Like the Buddha would instruct people with the gradual training, they would have to result.

283
00:23:23,237 --> 00:23:27,119
[SPEAKER_02]: In drying up, withdrawal, jhāna, right view jhāna, and so on.

284
00:23:27,259 --> 00:23:27,839
[SPEAKER_02]: You would have to.

285
00:23:28,419 --> 00:23:31,441
[SPEAKER_02]: So, if it's not, oh, it's because you haven't completed this first.

286
00:23:31,581 --> 00:23:38,964
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you're trying to to build the second and the third floor, and so on, while the the basis is still, like, lacking, not complete.

287
00:23:44,625 --> 00:23:52,613
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, it's the danger of, like you just said, the danger of losing that kind of perspective and that sense of that danger.

288
00:23:52,633 --> 00:23:54,876
[SPEAKER_02]: That's the only thing you should be afraid of, by the way.

289
00:23:56,477 --> 00:23:57,519
[SPEAKER_02]: You don't have to fear anything else.

290
00:23:59,381 --> 00:23:59,621
[SPEAKER_02]: At all.

291
00:24:01,883 --> 00:24:03,104
[SPEAKER_02]: And it's like, actually, that's a good deal.

292
00:24:03,365 --> 00:24:04,946
[SPEAKER_02]: Instead of fearing everything and nothing,

293
00:24:05,772 --> 00:24:09,174
[SPEAKER_02]: I'll just fear this one thing, losing the mindfulness of the body on the right level.

294
00:24:09,554 --> 00:24:10,835
[SPEAKER_00]: Which you're completely responsible for.

295
00:24:10,875 --> 00:24:14,358
[SPEAKER_02]: Which is entirely always, like, in my control.

296
00:24:15,038 --> 00:24:17,640
[SPEAKER_02]: Whether I do it or not is because I'm intentionally doing it or not.

297
00:24:19,362 --> 00:24:21,783
[SPEAKER_02]: And if that's all you protect, well, that's all you need to protect.

298
00:24:22,104 --> 00:24:31,748
[SPEAKER_02]: Then, everything else that happens and so on, it's like, it won't be a fault, it won't be a blameworthy thing, because it's not done through the gateway of desire.

299
00:24:31,768 --> 00:24:38,431
[SPEAKER_02]: Gateway of desire being taking up the body ignorantly, through a distracted manner, as a basis for pleasure.

300
00:24:42,448 --> 00:24:52,092
[SPEAKER_01]: And something someone said in regards to that, which inspires, well, can inspire a lot of sense of urgency is, when you lose that perspective,

301
00:24:54,820 --> 00:24:58,901
[SPEAKER_01]: In whatever way, there is no guarantee that you are getting back to it.

302
00:24:59,701 --> 00:25:05,823
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you distract yourself, well, enough, you might be going down.

303
00:25:06,243 --> 00:25:08,544
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, you might run out of time in the meantime.

304
00:25:08,944 --> 00:25:12,105
[SPEAKER_02]: You might be able to get back if you were to have time, but you might not have time.

305
00:25:12,785 --> 00:25:16,106
[SPEAKER_02]: You don't, as I said, contemplating the unknown ability of the body.

306
00:25:16,126 --> 00:25:17,806
[SPEAKER_02]: So it's not like, oh, so what do I do next?

307
00:25:17,826 --> 00:25:18,386
[SPEAKER_02]: What do I do next?

308
00:25:18,406 --> 00:25:18,606
[SPEAKER_02]: No.

309
00:25:18,806 --> 00:25:19,607
[SPEAKER_02]: This is what you do.

310
00:25:19,627 --> 00:25:20,767
[SPEAKER_02]: You just don't fall from this.

311
00:25:23,020 --> 00:25:23,340
[SPEAKER_02]: That's it.

312
00:25:23,500 --> 00:25:26,042
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it is not like, oh, and then the next step, and then the next step.

313
00:25:26,082 --> 00:25:26,202
[SPEAKER_02]: No.

314
00:25:26,222 --> 00:25:27,983
[SPEAKER_02]: Just protect and purify this step.

315
00:25:29,384 --> 00:25:38,971
[SPEAKER_02]: As Satipaṭṭhāna Sutta says, or he practices mindfulness of the body, which I will translate it, to the extent necessary for the final knowledge.

316
00:25:40,972 --> 00:25:48,517
[SPEAKER_02]: So, he purifies the relationship between him and his own body, relinquishes it to the extent necessary for final knowledge.

317
00:25:49,398 --> 00:25:49,758
[SPEAKER_02]: That's it.

318
00:25:52,793 --> 00:25:55,835
[SPEAKER_04]: Arahantship, complete freedom, yeah.

319
00:25:56,756 --> 00:25:59,939
[SPEAKER_02]: Relinquishing all ownership, any base for suffering, done.

320
00:26:00,739 --> 00:26:04,882
[SPEAKER_02]: And then it says, oh, if he doesn't get the arahantship, this life, well, then at least be a non-longer-eternal.

321
00:26:06,183 --> 00:26:08,405
[SPEAKER_00]: There are these other suttas, yeah, where it says, like, very directly...

322
00:26:08,425 --> 00:26:12,228
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you gotta do that, keep that, just keep that, that your work's done.

323
00:26:13,189 --> 00:26:16,551
[SPEAKER_02]: And the results of arahantship and so on, will have to follow.

324
00:26:16,651 --> 00:26:18,733
[SPEAKER_02]: If not, well, at least anāgāmī will have to follow.

325
00:26:19,617 --> 00:26:25,882
[SPEAKER_02]: If that relationship is uprooted and relinquished on the right level and protected through sensory strength and gradual training.

326
00:26:31,653 --> 00:26:34,114
[SPEAKER_02]: So that, how long that has to be done, that's not up to you.

327
00:26:34,975 --> 00:26:41,679
[SPEAKER_02]: That's up to how much kilesa, how much obstructions you have had through the countless times of, you know, engaging with saṃsāra.

328
00:26:42,359 --> 00:26:47,262
[SPEAKER_02]: So, for some people, it would be, as the Buddha said, seven years of having to protect that.

329
00:26:47,762 --> 00:26:49,063
[SPEAKER_02]: Some people would be seven months.

330
00:26:49,303 --> 00:26:50,703
[SPEAKER_02]: Some people would be half a night.

331
00:26:50,984 --> 00:26:51,124
[SPEAKER_02]: But

332
00:26:51,844 --> 00:27:03,711
[SPEAKER_02]: As I always say, I think it's a safe bet that today, two and a half thousand years after the Buddha, we are probably on that longer end of that spectrum.

333
00:27:06,133 --> 00:27:06,873
[SPEAKER_02]: Seventeen years.

334
00:27:08,354 --> 00:27:10,956
[SPEAKER_02]: But again, it's pleasant abiding here and now.

335
00:27:10,996 --> 00:27:12,637
[SPEAKER_02]: It's not seventeen years of torture.

336
00:27:12,737 --> 00:27:19,541
[SPEAKER_02]: It's seventeen years of reminding yourself how you're debt-free, could you relinquish the basis of pleasure and any further borrowing.

337
00:27:20,301 --> 00:27:21,042
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it's a win-win.

338
00:27:22,552 --> 00:27:23,536
[SPEAKER_00]: You lose nothing, really.

339
00:27:23,596 --> 00:27:24,118
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

340
00:27:24,238 --> 00:27:26,286
[SPEAKER_00]: You rancor things, you lose absolutely nothing.

341
00:27:26,306 --> 00:27:26,888
[SPEAKER_02]: Exactly, yeah.

342
00:27:26,988 --> 00:27:27,630
[SPEAKER_01]: You cannot, yeah.

343
00:27:27,650 --> 00:27:28,333
[SPEAKER_01]: That's exactly it.

